% Goal

Implement `brain/collection_registry.py` as a pure-stdlib machine-local registry of known music collections and the last active collection.

% Contract

R1 (MUST): Persist collection_id, display_name, mount_base, data_dir, index_path, volume_label and last_used_at in a versioned JSON document under gitignored brain/data.
R2 (MUST): Use atomic same-directory replacement and preserve every non-active collection record when registering or activating another.
R3 (MUST): `active_index_path` returns the supplied legacy fallback when no registry exists, preserving existing installations without migration at startup.
R4 (MUST): Activation validates that the registered mount and SQLite file are currently available before changing active_collection_id.
R5 (MUST NOT): Startup must not prompt, scan volumes, create databases, or silently select a different collection when the last active collection is unavailable.
R6 (MUST NOT): Never store the machine-local active pointer inside a removable collection database.
R7 (MUST): Reject malformed registry data and duplicate collection identities with conflicting paths explicitly.

% Output

- Primary output: `brain/collection_registry.py`.
- Python 3.13+, stdlib only.

% Verification

- Run focused registry and collection-switch tests, including atomic preservation, restart default, malformed registry and missing-volume behavior.
- Run the full unittest suite and py_compile.
